WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.649
Think you know hyperspace in the Star Wars
universe?

00:00:02.649 --> 00:00:06.770
Think again — because Ahsoka is changing
everything we know about the concept of lightspeed

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:08.060
travel.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:12.230
Following an action-packed and fan service-filled
fifth installment, Ahsoka has returned to

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.480
Disney+ once more.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.109
This episode, appropriately titled "Part Six:
Far, Far Away," places the focus on Sabine

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:22.840
Wren, who has traveled to a new galaxy alongside
the villainous Morgan Elsbeth, Baylan Skoll,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.070
and Shin Hati.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.380
While the villains meet with Grand Admiral
Thrawn, Sabine embarks on a search for her

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.939
long-lost friend, Ezra Bridger, on an unfamiliar
world.

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:35.560
At the same time, Ahsoka Tano and Huyang travel
inside the mouth of a purrgil, venturing through

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.590
hyperspace to rescue Sabine and stop Thrawn's
sinister plan.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.270
We only see a brief scene of the two heroes
early in the episode, though it does raise

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.530
one big question.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:47.700
As the purrgils embark on their voyage to
a new galaxy, hyperspace takes on a very different

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.910
appearance to what Star Wars fans might know.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:54.170
Rather than its usual swirling white and blue
look, the simu-tunnel the purrgil pod moves

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.870
through is streaky and multicolored, featuring
green, orange, and indigo hues.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.920
While this is most likely how the minds behind
Ahsoka wanted to convey to viewers that the

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.740
purrgil are traveling to a different galaxy,
there could be more to this new hyperspace

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:06.740
look.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:07.740
"Punch it!"

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.530
Purrgil are among the most fascinating creatures
in the Star Wars universe, and not just because

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:13.869
of their look or backstory.

00:01:13.869 --> 00:01:16.820
Rather, it's their unique biology that makes
them so interesting.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.470
These massive space-faring whales breathe
in a gas called Clouzon-36, which their bodies

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.550
take in and turn into hypermatter fuel.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.720
When sufficiently fueled up, their tentacles
will glow, and they'll make the jump to hyperspace,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.830
venturing from galaxy to galaxy at their leisure
— until they need to take in more gas once

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:32.830
again.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.000
Everyone else in the Star Wars galaxy can
only jump through hyperspace using a ship

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.310
with an operational hyperdrive.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:41.870
In this case, their Clouzon-36-based fuel
is refined by other means — and certainly

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.829
not inside the body of a purrgil.

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:47.930
Thus, you could argue that the purrgil simu-tunnel
on Ahsoka looks different than the one previously

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:51.650
seen in Star Wars because it is unique to
the way in which the purrgil travel.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:55.900
Perhaps the way they synthesize Clouzon-36
inside their bodies results in hyperspace

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.360
looking different than it would if you used
synthetic hyperfuel?

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.520
Of course, for fans who are familiar with
Star Wars Rebels, it won't be anything new

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.270
to experience a more colorful form of hyperspace
travel.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:09.080
Even without the presence of purrgils, the
series depicts multicolored tunnels on multiple

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.080
occasions.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.319
For example, in the Season 1 episode "Gathering
Forces," the Ghost accidentally jumps into

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:17.070
hyperspace courtesy of a Rodian agent named
Tseebo.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:20.900
After an Imperial tracker is placed on the
ship's auxiliary vessel, the Phantom, Ezra

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:25.060
Bridger and Kanan Jarrus concoct a plan to
detach it during a hyperspace jump.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.380
The risky move pays off, but not without a
bumpy and very vibrant fall first.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:33.220
In the following season, Rebels fans witnessed
a similar sight during the events of "Stealth

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:34.220
Strike."

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.550
Traveling through hyperspace in a blockade
runner, Ezra and Commander Sato are confronted

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:41.360
by a dangerous Imperial weapon: an Interdictor-class
Star Destroyer.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.080
The experimental ship is capable of pulling
other ships out of hyperspace, something it

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.750
does with ease to the heroes' own vessel.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:50.870
As they lose control of their ship, the blues
and whites of hyperspace are replaced by streaks

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.330
of red, yellow, and violet.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.340
Of course, eagle-eyed fans might have noticed
that the villains' hyperspace journey in Episode

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.159
6 was nearly as colorful as that taken by
the purrgil.

00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:02.840
This could mean that a vibrant simu-tunnel
is merely a quirk of this new galaxy — or

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.950
that the Eye of Sion somehow utilizes the
purrgil's abilities to travel into the unknown.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:11.730
All of this hyperspace confusion just goes
to show that Star Wars space travel is far

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.360
from an exact science.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.050
Maybe someday, a project will come along that
better explains the ins and outs of hyperspace

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.959
— but for now, all we can do is speculate
on the truth behind its many colors.

